{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R1-pending-b — over WebSocket, each binary message carries exactly one length-prefixed frame.",
    "",
    "Batch 2. §9.1's \"Wire framing (§1.6)\" row covers §1.6 wholesale, and §1.6 carries a TCP frame",
    "definition AND binding WebSocket MUSTs. Routed as a bundle (F15), not split here."
  ],
  "id_status": "pending-split",
  "under": "ECP-R1",
  "split_index": "b",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F15",
  "title": "Over WebSocket, every binary message the peer sends contains exactly one complete length-prefixed frame",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.6 (WebSocket transport)",
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "reading": [
    "§1.6: \"Each WebSocket binary message MUST contain exactly one length-prefixed frame. Implementations",
    "MUST NOT split a frame across multiple WebSocket messages or batch multiple frames into a single",
    "WebSocket message.\" And for stream adapters: \"MUST ensure each frame write produces a single WebSocket",
    "message.\"",
    "",
    "THREE MUST SENTENCES, ONE OBLIGATION. \"Exactly one\" is the conjunction of \"not fewer\" (no split) and",
    "\"not more\" (no batch); the second sentence restates the first by its two failure modes, and the third",
    "restates it for one implementation technique. They fail together — there is no peer that satisfies",
    "\"exactly one\" and violates \"no split\". So one file, two refusals.",
    "",
    "OBSERVABLE ON THE PEER'S OWN OUTPUT ONLY. The suite can inspect the WebSocket messages the peer",
    "SENDS. What the peer does with a split or batched INBOUND message is not stated (§1.6 gives receivers",
    "a MAY — \"MAY validate that the WebSocket message length equals 4 + the length prefix value\" — and",
    "no MUST), so no arm sends malformed messages and expects a particular refusal.",
    "",
    "VACUOUS ON A PEER THAT OFFERS NO WEBSOCKET TRANSPORT. §1.6 does not make WebSocket mandatory. A peer",
    "with no WebSocket listener has no messages to inspect; the run reports SKIP naming the absent",
    "transport, which is a declared precondition, not a pass.",
    "",
    "BATCHING NEEDS PRESSURE TO BE SEEN. A peer that writes through a buffered stream adapter batches only",
    "when two frames are written close together. The conformant arm therefore provokes several responses",
    "in quick succession (pipelined requests); a single request-response exchange cannot fail on the",
    "batching half."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "transport"
    ],
    "note": "transport = websocket, with the peer's WebSocket endpoint declared. Absent endpoint -> SKIP naming it."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "one-frame-per-message",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a WebSocket connection; a handshake, then N >= 8 EXECUTEs sent pipelined without awaiting responses",
      "why": "§1.6's MUST on the peer's output, under the write pressure that exposes batching.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "ws_open"
        },
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_pipelined_executes",
          "count": 8,
          "capture": "responses"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "ws_each_message_is_one_frame",
          "capture": "responses",
          "why": "for every binary message received: message length == 4 + big-endian prefix. Covers both failure modes in one comparison."
        }
      ],
      "refuse": [
        {
          "outcome": "message_shorter_than_frame",
          "why": "a split frame."
        },
        {
          "outcome": "message_longer_than_frame",
          "why": "batched frames."
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "text_message_count",
          "why": "a text-mode message is ECP-R1-pending-c's failure and is scored THERE ONLY — §1.6's 'exactly one length-prefixed frame' sentence governs binary messages. Recorded here so a suite implementing only this file does not read a text frame as a pass; never scored twice. (Corrected 2026-09-12, review: this was a refuse, which scored one behaviour in two files.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the suite's own recording of a fixture stream in which two frames are concatenated into one binary message",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY — and a SUITE-SIDE control, because no conformant peer can be asked to batch. Proves the assertion can fire: a suite whose comparison is written wrong (e.g. checks only the first frame) passes every peer, and only a fixture it must reject shows otherwise.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "replay_fixture",
          "fixture": "two valid frames in one WebSocket binary message",
          "capture": "fixture"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "ws_each_message_is_one_frame",
          "capture": "fixture",
          "expect": "fails"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that wraps its TCP stream writer around a WebSocket connection with an adapter that flushes on buffer fill",
      "expect": "under pipelined load, two or more frames arrive in one binary message",
      "why": "the adapter class §1.6's third sentence was written for."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "",
    "oracle_note": "none in `connectivity`. Unsearched beyond the category — bounded."
  }
}
